1
00:00:00,099 --> 00:00:03,988
What happens when a brand-new database says "Full ACID"...

2
00:00:03,988 --> 00:00:07,800
and then gets tested by Jepsen?

3
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:09,260
Ninety-six bugs.

4
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:14,380
Jepsen is the group that stress-tests databases until they break.

5
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:17,662
This time, the target was ParticleDB,

6
00:00:17,662 --> 00:00:18,730
a new database

7
00:00:18,730 --> 00:00:21,850
built for AI workloads, written in Rust,

8
00:00:21,850 --> 00:00:27,047
and promising to be a thousand times faster than Postgres.

9
00:00:27,047 --> 00:00:29,860
And the bugs weren't subtle.

10
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:31,340
You didn't need a cluster.

11
00:00:31,340 --> 00:00:33,040
You didn't need failures.

12
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:35,550
One server, one client was enough.

13
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:38,300
Ask the database "where four equals five,"

14
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:40,947
and it hands you back a row.

15
00:00:40,947 --> 00:00:44,230
Tell it a column can't be null,

16
00:00:44,230 --> 00:00:47,640
and it happily stores a null.

17
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:51,280
Delete a row? Sometimes it just refuses to die.

18
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:55,185
Drop a table? Its rows can come back from the dead.

19
00:00:55,185 --> 00:00:58,710
Even after most bugs were fixed,

20
00:00:58,710 --> 00:01:03,435
the default mode still lost committed writes.

21
00:01:03,435 --> 00:01:05,780
Data the database said was safely saved...

22
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:08,747
was just gone.

23
00:01:08,747 --> 00:01:11,690
Jepsen's advice was blunt:

24
00:01:11,690 --> 00:01:15,100
stop deploying it, and move to something else.

25
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:18,960
The author also points at a bigger question:

26
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:23,600
the team used AI tools to build this at incredible speed.

27
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:25,540
But when the bugs showed up,

28
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:29,350
the team often couldn't explain why they happened,

29
00:01:29,350 --> 00:01:31,840
or how they were fixed.

30
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:38,970
Fast to build is not the same as safe to trust.

31
00:01:38,970 --> 00:01:40,760
Especially when it's your data.
